A||||4||redfiero||Plenum & Valve Cover Finish||||1||
Z||000000||redfiero||10-03-2001||07:42 AM||||Has anyone tried to strip and polish their intake plenum and valve covers?  Got any pictures?  How much work was the polishing?<P>What about anodizing?  Does the anodizing chip or peel when you take the bolts in and out?<P>When you repaint your valve covers, did you tape off the unfinished fins or strip the paint afterwards.<P>I'm just looking for ideas.<P>Thanks,<BR>matt||||reg||1
Z||000001||SKIDMARK||10-03-2001||09:18 AM||||There is a guy in the Ceveland club who just polished and buffed his plenum and it looked pretty nice. He said it wasn't hard but I guess that would depend on the polishing equipment and your skill level. Personally I plan on stripping and painting mine and then remachining the ribs. I found some hi-temp clearcoat to finish the job. I also found an engraver who will do the "FIERO" on the plenum for $20 to eliminate the hoaky sticker.||||reg||1
Z||000002||StuGood||10-03-2001||09:42 AM||||On my GT, I stripped the finish off the plenum and valve covers using "aircraft remover" (sounds like something you would use to clear a runway?   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif">), then used some of that "high-temp engine paint" you find at the local auto parts store.  I only masked the openings in the plenum (to keep paint from getting on the inside), and vacuum fittings.   Applied a 2nd coat after it dried.<BR>--------<BR>Edit: I forgot to mention, after each coat of color, I carefully applied some (old, used) brake fluid to the fins, to soften / strip off the paint...      <BR>--------<BR>Next, I sanded the paint off the fins (left the Swedes alone   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif">), and also hand-sanded them with wet/dry sandpaper, to polish them a bit.  After that, sprayed with clear "high temp engine paint," to give it some shine.<P>I am very pleased with the result.  Didn't cost much, money- nor time-wise, and it looks very sharp.  Hardest part was picking out the color   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">    <p>[This message has been edited by StuGood (edited 10-03-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000003||Dan87GT||10-03-2001||10:59 AM||||I did the same thing as Stu, just make sure you sand the ribs within a day or two so the paint isn't fully dried and make sure you start out with a fine grade sandpaper so you don't flake the paint off at the edges. Mine turned out better than new...||||reg||1
Z||000004||Alex4mula||10-03-2001||11:31 AM||||When I did my valve covers I put light coat of vaseline on the fins. Once paint dryed a little I used a razor knife to peel the vaseline with the paint at the top. Easier than sanding and the result was very good. Just another option.||||reg||1
